      "id": 1,
      "text": "Winchester was incorporated in 1842, taking land from Woburn, Medford, and West Cambridge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.winchester.us/480/Winchester-History-Online",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Middlesex Canal opened in 1803, but the Boston and Lowell Railroad, completed in 1835, soon replaced it and reshaped the village.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.winchester.us/480/Winchester-History-Online",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Winchester High School, founded in 1850, began as part of the present‑day Lincoln Elementary building.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winchester%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Winchester Boat Club, established in 1900 as a canoe club, still welcomes locals to sail on the Mystic Lakes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winchester%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town’s historic buildings, many dating back to its early industrial era, give Winchester a distinctive old‑world charm.",
